AMC.add({"comp": "amc10", "id": "2022B", "label": "2022 B", "problems": [{"q": "<p> Define <span class=\"tex\">x \\diamond y</span> to be <span class=\"tex\">|x-y|</span> for all real numbers <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span>. What is the value of</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(1 \\diamond(2 \\diamond 3))-((1 \\diamond 2) \\diamond 3)?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">-2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">-1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">0</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>First observe that <span class=\"tex\">2 \\diamond 3=|2-3|=|-1|=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">1 \\diamond 2=|1-2|=|-1|=1</span>. Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(1 \\diamond(2 \\diamond 3))-((1 \\diamond 2) \\diamond 3)=1 \\diamond 1-1 \\diamond 3=|1-1|-|1-3|=|0|-|-2|=(\\text{A})\\boxed{-2} .</span></p><p>Note: This shows that the <span class=\"tex\">\\diamond</span> operation is not associative.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-1"}, {"q": "<p> In rhombus <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span>, point <span class=\"tex\">P</span> lies on segment <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A D}</span> such that <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B P} \\perp \\overline{A D}, A P=3</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">P D=2</span>. What is the area of <span class=\"tex\">A B C D?</span></p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc10/2022B/2024_06_20_3519e670deae788d110bg-175.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">3 \\sqrt{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6 \\sqrt{5}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">20</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">25</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>The side length of the rhombus is <span class=\"tex\">A P+P D=5</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">A B=5</span>. The Pythagorean Theorem applied to <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B P A</span> yields <span class=\"tex\">B P=4</span>. Thus the area of <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> is <span class=\"tex\">5 \\cdot 4=(\\text{D})\\boxed{20}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-2"}, {"q": "<p> How many three-digit positive integers have an odd number of even digits?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">150</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">250</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">350</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">450</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">550</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>There are <span class=\"tex\">9 \\cdot 10=90</span> choices for the hundreds and tens digits of a three-digit positive integer. For each such choice, exactly 5 of the 10 possible choices for the units digit will make the total number of even digits odd. Therefore the number of three-digit positive integers with an odd number of even digits is <span class=\"tex\">90 \\cdot 5=(\\text{D})\\boxed{450}</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>A three-digit positive integer that has an odd number of even digits must have either three even digits or one even digit. There are <span class=\"tex\">4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5=100</span> ways for all the digits to be even, because the leading digit cannot be 0 . If there is exactly one even digit, that digit can occur in either the hundreds, the tens, or the ones place. These cases, respectively, give <span class=\"tex\">4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5+5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5+5 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 5=350</span> additional possibilities. Hence the number of three-digit positive integers with an odd number of even digits is <span class=\"tex\">100+350=(\\text{D})\\boxed{450}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-3"}, {"q": "<p> A donkey suffers an attack of hiccups and the first hiccup happens at <span class=\"tex\">4:00</span> one afternoon. Suppose that the donkey hiccups regularly every <span class=\"tex\">5</span> seconds. At what time does the donkey's <span class=\"tex\">700</span>th hiccup occur?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">15</span> seconds after <span class=\"tex\">4: 58</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">20</span> seconds after <span class=\"tex\">4: 58</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">25</span> seconds after <span class=\"tex\">4: 58</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">30</span> seconds after <span class=\"tex\">4: 58</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">35</span> seconds after <span class=\"tex\">4: 58</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>There are 699 five-second time periods between the 1st hiccup and the 700th hiccup. This will require <span class=\"tex\">699 \\cdot 5=3495</span> seconds. Because there are <span class=\"tex\">60 \\cdot 60=3600</span> seconds in an hour, the 700 th hiccup will occur <span class=\"tex\">3600-3495=105</span> seconds before 5:00. This is <span class=\"tex\">105-60=45</span> seconds before <span class=\"tex\">4: 59</span>, which is <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{A})\\boxed{15 seconds after 4:58}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-4"}, {"q": "<p> What is the value of</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{\\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)\\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)\\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right)}{\\sqrt{\\left(1-\\dfrac{1}{3^{2}}\\right)\\left(1-\\dfrac{1}{5^{2}}\\right)\\left(1-\\dfrac{1}{7^{2}}\\right)}}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{15}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{105}</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">x</span> be the displayed expression. Then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &amp; =\\frac{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{2}\\left(1+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^{2}\\left(1+\\frac{1}{7}\\right)^{2}}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{3^{2}}\\right)\\left(1-\\frac{1}{5^{2}}\\right)\\left(1-\\frac{1}{7^{2}}\\right)} \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\cdot \\frac{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\cdot \\frac{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)} \\cdot \\frac{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)} \\cdot \\frac{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{7}\\right)} \\cdot \\frac{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)} \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\cdot \\frac{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)} \\cdot \\frac{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)} \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{4}{2} \\cdot \\frac{6}{4} \\cdot \\frac{8}{6}=4 .\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Therefore <span class=\"tex\">x=(\\text{B})\\boxed{2}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-5"}, {"q": "<p> How many of the first ten numbers of the sequence <span class=\"tex\">121,11211,1112111, \\ldots</span> are prime numbers?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">0</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>Note that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n121 &amp; =110+11=11 \\cdot(10+1), \\\\\n11211 &amp; =11100+111=111 \\cdot(100+1), \\\\\n1112111 &amp; =1111000+1111=1111 \\cdot(1000+1),\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>and so on. Therefore each number in the sequence can be written as a product of two positive integers, each greater than 1 , so none of the numbers in this sequence is prime.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-6"}, {"q": "<p> For how many values of the constant <span class=\"tex\">k</span> will the polynomial <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+k x+36</span> have two distinct integer roots?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">14</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">16</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>In order for the given polynomial to have two distinct integer roots, the polynomial must factor as the product <span class=\"tex\">(x+a)(x+b)</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> are integers, with <span class=\"tex\">a b=36</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a \\neq b</span>. In each case, <span class=\"tex\">k=a+b</span>. The choices for <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> are then <span class=\"tex\">(1,36),(-1,-36),(2,18),(-2,-18),(3,12)</span>, <span class=\"tex\">(-3,-12),(4,9)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">(-4,-9)</span>, yielding <span class=\"tex\">k=37,-37,20,-20,15,-15,13</span>, and -13 , respectively. Thus there are <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{B})\\boxed{8}</span> values for <span class=\"tex\">k</span>. (Note that the ordered pairs <span class=\"tex\">(a, b)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">(b, a)</span> produce the same value of <span class=\"tex\">k</span>, so the pairs <span class=\"tex\">(36,1),(-36,-1),(18,2),(-18,-2)</span> and so forth can be ignored.)</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-7"}, {"q": "<p> Consider the following <span class=\"tex\">100</span> sets of <span class=\"tex\">10</span> elements each:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{array}{l}\n\\{1,2,3,\\ldots,10\\}, \\\\\n\\{11,12,13,\\ldots,20\\},\\\\\n\\{21,22,23,\\ldots,30\\},\\\\\n\\\\\n\\vdots \\\\\n\\{991,992,993,\\ldots,1000\\}.\n\\end{array}</span></p><p>How many of these sets contain exactly two multiples of <span class=\"tex\">7?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">40</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">42</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">42</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">49</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">50</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>Observe that 1000 divided by 7 is 142 with a remainder of 6 . There are therefore 142 positive multiples of 7 that are less than 1000 , namely <span class=\"tex\">7,14,21,28, \\ldots, 994</span>. Because every set of 10 consecutive integers must contain at least one multiple of 7 and cannot contain three multiples of 7 , exactly <span class=\"tex\">142-100=(\\text{B})\\boxed{42}</span> of the given sets must contain exactly two multiples of 7 .</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-8"}, {"q": "<p> The sum</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\dfrac{1}{2!}+\\dfrac{2}{3!}+\\dfrac{3}{4!}+\\cdots+\\dfrac{2021}{2022!}</span></p><p>can be expressed as <span class=\"tex\">a-\\dfrac{1}{b!}</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> are positive integers. What is <span class=\"tex\">a+b?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2020</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2021</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2022</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2023</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2024</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Notice that for <span class=\"tex\">n \\geq 2</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{n-1}{n!}=\\frac{1}{(n-1)!}-\\frac{1}{n!} .</span></p><p>Therefore the given sum telescopes:</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{1}{1!}-\\frac{1}{2!}+\\frac{1}{2!}-\\frac{1}{3!}+\\frac{1}{3!}-\\frac{1}{4!}+\\cdots+\\frac{1}{2021!}-\\frac{1}{2022!}=1-\\frac{1}{2022!}</span></p><p>Thus <span class=\"tex\">a=1, b=2022</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">a+b=(\\text{D})\\boxed{2023}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-9"}, {"q": "<p> Camila writes down five positive integers. The unique mode of these integers is <span class=\"tex\">2</span> greater than their median, and the median is <span class=\"tex\">2</span> greater than their arithmetic mean. What is the least possible value for the mode?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">9</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">11</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">13</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Assume for simplicity that the five integers are written in nondecreasing order. Let <span class=\"tex\">c</span> be the unique mode of these integers. Because the mode is 2 greater than the third number, the three greatest integers must be <span class=\"tex\">c-2, c</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c</span>, in that order. Let <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> be the two least integers. Because the arithmetic mean of the five integers equals <span class=\"tex\">c-4</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">a+b+(c-2)+c+c=5(c-4),</span></p><p>which can be rewritten as <span class=\"tex\">2 c=a+b+18</span>. Because the mode is unique, <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and <span class=\"tex\">b</span> are distinct positive integers. This equation requires <span class=\"tex\">a+b</span> to be even, so the least possible value of <span class=\"tex\">c</span> occurs when <span class=\"tex\">a+b=1+3</span>, giving <span class=\"tex\">c=22 \\div 2=(\\text{D})\\boxed{11}</span>. This minimum mode is achieved with the integers <span class=\"tex\">1,3,9</span>, 11 , and 11 .</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-10"}, {"q": "<p> All the high schools in a large school district are involved in a fundraiser selling T-shirts. Which of the choices below is logically equivalent to the statement \"No school bigger than Euclid HS sold more T-shirts than Euclid HS\"?</p>", "c": ["All schools smaller than Euclid HS sold fewer T-shirts than Euclid HS.", "No school that sold more T-shirts than Euclid HS is bigger than Euclid HS.", "All schools bigger than Euclid HS sold fewer T-shirts than Euclid HS.", "All schools that sold fewer T-shirts than Euclid HS are smaller than Euclid HS.", "All schools smaller than Euclid HS sold more T-shirts than Euclid HS."], "a": "C", "s": "<p>Consider a plot in which the number of T-shirts sold ( <span class=\"tex\">y</span>-axis) is graphed against the school size ( <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-axis) and each of the high schools is assigned a point on this plot. Let <span class=\"tex\">(X, Y)</span> be the point assigned to Euclid HS. The statement given in the problem is that for every high school, if the <span class=\"tex\">x</span>-coordinate of its point on the plot is greater than <span class=\"tex\">X</span>, then the <span class=\"tex\">y</span>-coordinate of its point must be less than or equal to <span class=\"tex\">Y</span>; that is, \"If <span class=\"tex\">x&gt;X</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">y \\leq Y</span>.\"</p>\n<p>Statement (A) is equivalent to \"If <span class=\"tex\">x&lt;X</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">y&lt;Y</span>.\"<br/>\nStatement (B) is equivalent to \"If <span class=\"tex\">y&gt;Y</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">x \\leq X</span>.\"<br/>\nStatement (C) is equivalent to \"If <span class=\"tex\">x&gt;X</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">y&lt;Y</span>.\"<br/>\nStatement (D) is equivalent to \"If <span class=\"tex\">y&lt;Y</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">x&lt;X</span>.\"<br/>\nStatement (E) is equivalent to \"If <span class=\"tex\">x&lt;X</span>, then <span class=\"tex\">y&gt;Y</span>.\"</p>\n<p>Statement (B) is the contrapositive of the original statement and is therefore logically equivalent to it. All of the other choices have different meanings and can have different truth values. For example, assume <span class=\"tex\">X=1000</span> and <span class=\"tex\">Y=50</span>. If <span class=\"tex\">x=2000</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y=60</span>, then the original statement is false, but statements (A), (D), and (E) are vacuously true (the hypothesis is false in each case). If <span class=\"tex\">x=2000</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y=50</span>, then the original statement is true, but statement <span class=\"tex\">(\\mathbf{C})</span> is false.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Let <span class=\"tex\">L</span> be the collection of schools bigger than Euclid HS. Let <span class=\"tex\">M</span> be the collection of schools that sold more T-shirts than Euclid HS. The original statement is that there is no <span class=\"tex\">x \\in L</span> that is also in <span class=\"tex\">M</span>. In other words, <span class=\"tex\">L</span> and <span class=\"tex\">M</span> are disjoint. Statement (B) says that there is no <span class=\"tex\">x \\in M</span> that is also in <span class=\"tex\">L</span>, which is also a way of saying that <span class=\"tex\">M</span> and <span class=\"tex\">L</span> are disjoint. Choice ( <span class=\"tex\">\\mathbf{C}</span> ) is incorrect because there might be a school bigger than Euclid HS that sold the same number of T-shirts as Euclid HS. Choices (A), (D), and (E) are incorrect because if all the other schools are bigger than Euclid HS and sold more T-shirts than Euclid HS, then the original statement is false but choices (A), (D), and (E) are (vacuously) true.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-11"}, {"q": "<p> A pair of fair <span class=\"tex\">6</span> -sided dice is rolled <span class=\"tex\">n</span> times. What is the least value of <span class=\"tex\">n</span> such that the probability that the sum of the numbers face up on a roll equals <span class=\"tex\">7</span> at least once is greater than <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">3</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">4</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">5</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">6</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>There are <span class=\"tex\">6 \\cdot 6=36</span> equally likely outcomes for the numbers face up on the two dice. In 6 of those cases, the sum will be 7 , namely <span class=\"tex\">(1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">(6,1)</span>. Therefore the probability of getting a sum of 7 on any particular roll is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{6}{36}=\\frac{1}{6}</span>, and the probability of not getting a sum of 7 is <span class=\"tex\">1-\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{5}{6}</span>. Because the outcomes of successive rolls are independent, the probability of not getting a sum of 7 on any of <span class=\"tex\">n</span> rolls is <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^{n}</span>. It remains to determine the least value of <span class=\"tex\">n</span> such that this value is less than or equal to <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note that <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^{1}=\\frac{5}{6}&gt;\\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{1}{2},\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^{2}=\\frac{25}{36}&gt;\\frac{18}{36}=\\frac{1}{2}</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^{3}=\\frac{125}{216}&gt;\\frac{108}{216}=\\frac{1}{2}</span>. But <span class=\"tex\">\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^{4}=\\frac{625}{1296}&lt;</span> <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{648}{1296}=\\frac{1}{2}</span>, so the requested value of <span class=\"tex\">n</span> is <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{C})\\boxed{4}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-12"}, {"q": "<p> The positive difference between a pair of primes is equal to <span class=\"tex\">2</span>, and the positive difference between the cubes of the two primes is <span class=\"tex\">31106</span>. What is the sum of the digits of the least prime that is greater than those two primes?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">8</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">11</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">13</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">16</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">p</span> and <span class=\"tex\">p+2</span> be the two primes. Then <span class=\"tex\">(p+2)^{3}-p^{3}=31106</span>. Expanding and simplifying yields <span class=\"tex\">p^{2}+2 p=5183</span>. Adding 1 to both sides and factoring gives <span class=\"tex\">(p+1)^{2}=5184</span>. The fact that <span class=\"tex\">70^{2}=4900</span> suggests trying <span class=\"tex\">p=71</span>, and indeed that works. The next prime after the two primes 71 and 73 is 79 . The requested sum of digits is <span class=\"tex\">7+9=(\\text{E})\\boxed{16}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: A pair of primes is called twin primes if their positive difference equals 2. Twin primes become increasingly rare as one examines larger ranges, in keeping with the general tendency of gaps between adjacent primes to become larger as the numbers themselves get larger. However, it is unknown whether there are infinitely many twin primes (the Twin Prime Conjecture).</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-13"}, {"q": "<p> Suppose that <span class=\"tex\">S</span> is a subset of <span class=\"tex\">\\{1,2,3, \\ldots, 25\\}</span> such that the sum of any two (not necessarily distinct) elements of <span class=\"tex\">S</span> is never an element of <span class=\"tex\">S</span>. What is the maximum number of elements <span class=\"tex\">S</span> may contain?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">12</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">13</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">14</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">16</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">S=\\{1,3,5, \\ldots, 25\\}</span> consist of the 13 odd elements. Then the sum of two elements of <span class=\"tex\">S</span> is even, and hence is not in <span class=\"tex\">S</span>. (The set <span class=\"tex\">S=\\{13,14,15, \\ldots, 25\\}</span> is another subset of size 13 with the given property, because the sum of any two elements of this set is greater than or equal to 26.)</p>\n<p>To show that no larger set has the required property, suppose that the greatest element of <span class=\"tex\">S</span> is odd, say <span class=\"tex\">2 k+1</span>. Then for <span class=\"tex\">j=1,2, \\ldots, k</span>, both <span class=\"tex\">j</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2 k+1-j</span> cannot be elements of <span class=\"tex\">S</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">S</span> has at most <span class=\"tex\">k+1</span> elements in this case, which suffices because <span class=\"tex\">k \\leq 12</span>. Now suppose that the greatest element of <span class=\"tex\">S</span> is even, say <span class=\"tex\">2 k</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">k \\notin S</span>, and for <span class=\"tex\">j=1,2, \\ldots, k-1</span>, at most one of <span class=\"tex\">j</span> and <span class=\"tex\">2 k-j</span> is in <span class=\"tex\">S</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">S</span> has at most <span class=\"tex\">k</span> elements. Because <span class=\"tex\">k \\leq 12</span>, the size of <span class=\"tex\">S</span> does not exceed <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{B})\\boxed{13}</span> in this case as well.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-14"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">S_{n}</span> be the sum of the first <span class=\"tex\">n</span> term of an arithmetic sequence that has a common difference of <span class=\"tex\">2</span>. The quotient <span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{S_{3 n}}{S_{n}}</span> does not depend on <span class=\"tex\">n</span>. What is <span class=\"tex\">S_{20}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">340</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">360</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">380</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">400</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">420</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">a</span> be the first term of the arithmetic sequence. The sum of the first <span class=\"tex\">n</span> terms of an arithmetic sequence with common difference 2 is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">S_{n}=\\frac{n}{2}(a+(a+(n-1) \\cdot 2))=n(a+n-1) .</span></p><p>Because <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{S_{3 n}}{S_{n}}</span> does not depend on <span class=\"tex\">n</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{S_{3}}{S_{1}}=\\frac{S_{6}}{S_{2}}</span>. This means that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{3(a+2)}{a}=\\frac{6(a+5)}{2(a+1)} .</span></p><p>Solving this equation gives <span class=\"tex\">a=1</span>.</p>\n<p>Indeed, if the first term of an arithmetic sequence is 1 and its common difference is 2, then</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{S_{3 n}}{S_{n}}=\\frac{3 n(1+3 n-1)}{n(1+n-1)}=9</span></p><p>which does not depend on <span class=\"tex\">n</span>. Therefore there is one and only one arithmetic sequence that satisfies the given properties, namely <span class=\"tex\">1,3,5, \\ldots</span> Thus <span class=\"tex\">S_{20}=20(1+20-1)=(\\text{D})\\boxed{400}</span>. In fact, <span class=\"tex\">S_{n}=n^{2}</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>As in the first solution, the sum of an arithmetic sequence with first term <span class=\"tex\">a</span> and common difference 2 is <span class=\"tex\">S_{n}=n(n+a-1)</span>. Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\frac{S_{3 n}}{S_{n}}=\\frac{(3 n)(3 n+a-1)}{n(n+a-1)}=3 \\cdot \\frac{3 n+a-1}{n+a-1}=9 \\cdot \\frac{1+\\frac{a-1}{3 n}}{1+\\frac{a-1}{n}} .</span></p><p>This value approaches 9 as <span class=\"tex\">n \\rightarrow \\infty</span>, so if <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{S_{3 n}}{S_{n}}</span> does not depend on <span class=\"tex\">n</span>, then it must be identically 9 and <span class=\"tex\">a</span> must equal 1. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">S_{20}=20 \\cdot(20+1-1)=(\\text{D})\\boxed{400}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-15"}, {"q": "<p> The diagram below shows a rectangle with side lengths <span class=\"tex\">4</span> and <span class=\"tex\">8</span> and a square with side length <span class=\"tex\">5</span>. Three vertices of the square lie on three different sides of the rectangle, as shown. What is the area of the region inside both the square and the rectangle?</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc10/2022B/2024_06_20_3519e670deae788d110bg-177.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">15 \\dfrac{1}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15 \\dfrac{3}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15 \\dfrac{1}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15 \\dfrac{5}{8}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15 \\dfrac{7}{8}</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Label the square counterclockwise <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span>, where <span class=\"tex\">D</span> is the vertex outside the rectangle, and let <span class=\"tex\">E</span> be the intersection of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span> with the top side of the rectangle. Let <span class=\"tex\">F</span> be the projection of <span class=\"tex\">A</span> onto the side of the rectangle containing <span class=\"tex\">B</span>, as shown.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc10/2022B/2025_01_28_64e1587309b5a3771dbdg-091_1_.jpg\" width=\"400\"/></div>\n<p>Then the side lengths of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A F B</span> are <span class=\"tex\">A B=5, A F=4</span>, and by the Pythagorean Theorem <span class=\"tex\">B F=3</span>. By alternate interior angles, <span class=\"tex\">\\angle F B A \\cong \\angle B A E \\cong \\angle A E D</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A D E \\sim \\triangle A F B</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">D E=</span> <span class=\"tex\">A D \\cdot \\frac{B F}{A F}=\\frac{15}{4}</span>. It follows that the area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A D E</span> is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 5 \\cdot \\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{75}{8}</span>. The requested area of the region inside both the square and the rectangle is the area of the square minus the area of <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle A D E</span>, which is <span class=\"tex\">5^{2}-\\frac{75}{8}=25-9 \\frac{3}{8}=(\\text{D})\\boxed{15 \\frac{5}{8}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-16"}, {"q": "<p> One of the following numbers is not divisible by any prime number less than <span class=\"tex\">10</span>. Which is it?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">2^{606}-1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2^{606}+1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2^{607}-1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2^{607}+1</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">2^{607}+3^{607}</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>Recall that if <span class=\"tex\">n</span> is an integer greater than 1 , then <span class=\"tex\">a^{n}-b^{n}</span> factors as</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(a-b)\\left(a^{n-1}+a^{n-2} b^{1}+a^{n-3} b^{2}+\\cdots+a^{1} b^{n-2}+b^{n-1}\\right) \\tag{*}</span></p><p>and, furthermore, if <span class=\"tex\">n</span> is odd, then <span class=\"tex\">a^{n}+b^{n}</span> factors as</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">(a+b)\\left(a^{n-1}-a^{n-2} b^{1}+a^{n-3} b^{2}-\\cdots-a^{1} b^{n-2}+b^{n-1}\\right)</span></p><p>For (A), observe that <span class=\"tex\">2^{606}-1=\\left(2^{3}\\right)^{202}-\\left(1^{3}\\right)^{202}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">2^{606}-1</span> is divisible by <span class=\"tex\">7=2^{3}-1</span> by <span class=\"tex\">(*)</span>. For (E), it follows from ( <span class=\"tex\">\\dagger</span> ) that <span class=\"tex\">2^{607}+3^{607}</span> is divisible by 5 . For ( <span class=\"tex\">\\mathbf{B}</span> ), because the units digits of successive powers of 2 cycle in groups of 4 , the units digit of <span class=\"tex\">2^{606}</span> is 4 . Therefore <span class=\"tex\">2^{606}+1</span> is divisible by 5 . For <span class=\"tex\">(\\mathbf{D})</span>, note that because <span class=\"tex\">2^{6} \\equiv 1(\\bmod 3)</span>, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">2^{606}=\\left(2^{6}\\right)^{101} \\equiv 1^{101} \\equiv 1</span> <span class=\"tex\">(\\bmod 3)</span>. Therefore <span class=\"tex\">2^{607}+1 \\equiv 2 \\cdot 1+1 \\equiv 0(\\bmod 3)</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">2^{607}+1</span> is divisible by 3 .</p>\n<p>Because it is given that one of the answer choices is not divisible by any prime number less than 10, the correct choice must be ( <span class=\"tex\">\\mathbf{C}</span> ). It can be verified, using the methods discussed above, that none of <span class=\"tex\">2,3,5</span>, or 7 divides <span class=\"tex\">(\\text{C})\\boxed{2^{607}-1}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: In fact, <span class=\"tex\">2^{607}-1</span> is the 14 th Mersenne prime, proven to be prime in 1952 .</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-17"}, {"q": "<p> Consider systems of three linear equations with unknowns <span class=\"tex\">x, y</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">z</span>,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\na_{1}x + b_{1}y + c_{1}z &amp;= 0 \\\\\na_{2}x + b_{2}y + c_{2}z &amp;= 0 \\\\\na_{3}x + b_{3}y + c_{3}z &amp;= 0\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>where each of the coefficients is either <span class=\"tex\">0</span> or <span class=\"tex\">1</span> and the system has a solution other than <span class=\"tex\">x=y=z=0</span>. For example, one such system is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\{1 x+1 y+0 z=0,0 x+1 y+1 z=0,0 x+0 y+0 z=0\\}</span></p><p>with a nonzero solution of <span class=\"tex\">\\{x, y, z\\}=\\{1,-1,1\\}</span>. How many such systems are there? (The equations in a system need not be distinct, and two systems containing the same equations in a different order are considered different.)</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">302</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">338</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">340</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">343</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">344</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>First consider systems in which one or more of the equations is <span class=\"tex\">0=0</span>. There is <span class=\"tex\">\\underline{1}</span> such system in which all three equations are <span class=\"tex\">0=0</span>. If exactly one of the equations is <span class=\"tex\">0=0</span>, then the other two equations can be any of <span class=\"tex\">2^{3}-1=7</span> possibilities and <span class=\"tex\">0=0</span> can occur in any of the 3 positions in the system, for <span class=\"tex\">7 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 3=\\underline{147}</span> possibilities. Similarly, if exactly two of the equations are <span class=\"tex\">0=0</span>, then the other equation can be any of 7 possibilities and can occur in any of the 3 positions in the system, for <span class=\"tex\">7 \\cdot 3=\\underline{21}</span> possibilities.</p>\n<p>From now on assume that none of the equations is <span class=\"tex\">0=0</span>. There are <span class=\"tex\">\\underline{\\underline{x}}</span> possibilities in which all three equations are the same, and <span class=\"tex\">7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 3=\\underline{126}</span> possibilities in which exactly two are the same.</p>\n<p>From now on assume further that all three equations are different. If one of the three variables is missing from all the equations, then, up to a permutation of variables or equations, the system must be <span class=\"tex\">\\langle x+y=0, x=0, y=0\\rangle</span>; there are <span class=\"tex\">3 \\cdot 6=\\underline{18}</span> systems of this type. Otherwise, all three variables are present and up to a permutation of variables or equations the system must be <span class=\"tex\">\\langle x+y+z=0, x+y=0, z=0\\rangle</span>, and there are <span class=\"tex\">3 \\cdot 6=\\underline{18}</span> systems of this type. The requested total is <span class=\"tex\">1+147+21+7+126+18+18=(\\text{B})\\boxed{338}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: The question is equivalent to asking how many <span class=\"tex\">3 \\times 3</span> matrices all of whose entries are 0 or 1 have determinant 0 . The corresponding answer for systems of four homogeneous linear equations with four unknowns (or <span class=\"tex\">4 \\times 4</span> matrices) is 42,976 . In each case, the fraction of systems with a nontrivial solution is <span class=\"tex\">66 \\%</span> to the nearest whole percent.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-18"}, {"q": "<p> Each square in a <span class=\"tex\">5 \\times 5</span> grid is either filled or empty, and has up to eight adjacent neighboring squares, where neighboring squares share either a side or a corner. The grid is transformed by the following rules:</p>\n<p>Any filled square with two or three filled neighbors remains filled. Any empty square with exactly three filled neighbors becomes a filled square. All other squares remain empty or become empty. A sample transformation is shown in the figure below.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc10/2022B/2024_06_20_4032a3f61deaf5058cd0g-177_2_.jpg\" width=\"\"/>\n<img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc10/2022B/2024_06_20_4032a3f61deaf5058cd0g-177_3_.jpg\" width=\"\"/>\n</div>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">\\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad</span> <strong>Initial</strong> <span class=\"tex\">\\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad</span> <strong>Transformed</strong></p>\n<p>Suppose the <span class=\"tex\">5 \\times 5</span> grid has a border of empty squares surrounding a <span class=\"tex\">3 \\times 3</span> subgrid. How many initial configurations will lead to a transformed grid consisting of a single filled square in the center after a single transformation? (Rotations and reflections of the same configuration are considered different.)</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\">\n<img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc10/2022B/2024-07-22_203856.png\" width=\"\"/>\n<img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc10/2022B/2024_06_20_4032a3f61deaf5058cd0g-177_1_.jpg\" width=\"\"/>\n</div>\n<p><span class=\"tex\">\\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad\\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad</span> <strong>Initial</strong> <span class=\"tex\">\\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad</span> <strong>Transformed</strong></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">14</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">18</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">22</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">26</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">30</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>If the center square is filled, then in order for it to remain filled either two or three of the eight neighboring squares must be filled. If two of these are in the middle horizontal or vertical row then a noncentral square would become filled. Now suppose that exactly one of these is in the middle horizontal or vertical row and there is one other square that is filled. By symmetry, there are only two cases to consider, and in each of these cases a noncentral square would become filled. If exactly one of these filled squares is in the middle horizontal or vertical row and there are two other squares that are filled, a noncentral square would also become filled. Therefore the only possibility in this case is the one shown in the first diagram below or a rotation of that diagram.</p>\n<p>If the center square is not filled, then in order for it to become filled exactly three of the eight neighboring squares must be filled. The only case (up to rotation) in which two of them are diagonally adjacent is shown in the second diagram below. The two cases up to rotation in which none of these three filled-in squares are adjacent are shown in the next two diagrams below. Finally,</p>\n<p>if two squares of the filled-in squares are adjacent along an edge, then the configuration must look like the fifth diagram below, up to rotation or reflection.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc10/2022B/2025_01_28_64e1587309b5a3771dbdg-095_1_.jpg\" width=\"700\"/></div>\n<p>By symmetry, the first pattern corresponds to 2 configurations, the next 3 patterns correspond to 4 configurations each, and the last pattern corresponds to 8 configurations, for a total of <span class=\"tex\">1 \\cdot 2+3</span>. <span class=\"tex\">4+1 \\cdot 8=(\\text{C})\\boxed{22}</span> configurations.</p>\n<p>Note: This problem is based on the cellular automaton Game of Life, created by John Conway (1937-2020).</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-19"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">A B C D</span> be a rhombus with <span class=\"tex\">\\angle A D C=46^{\\circ}</span>. Let <span class=\"tex\">E</span> be the midpoint of <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{C D}</span>, and let <span class=\"tex\">F</span> be the point on <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B E}</span> such that <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A F}</span> is perpendicular to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{B E}</span>. What is the degree measure of <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B F C?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">110</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">111</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">112</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">113</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">114</span>"], "a": "D", "s": "<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">G</span> be the intersection point of lines <span class=\"tex\">A D</span> and <span class=\"tex\">B E</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle B C E</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\triangle G D E</span> are congruent. Points <span class=\"tex\">A, C</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">G</span> lie on a circle with center at <span class=\"tex\">D</span> because <span class=\"tex\">A D=G D=C D</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A F}</span> is perpendicular to <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{G F}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">\\overline{A G}</span> is a diameter of the circle, it follows that point <span class=\"tex\">F</span> lies on the circle. Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\angle G F C=\\frac{1}{2} \\angle G D C=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(180^{\\circ}-46^{\\circ}\\right)=67^{\\circ}</span></p><p>Therefore <span class=\"tex\">\\angle B F C=180^{\\circ}-\\angle G F C=(\\text{D})\\boxed{113^{\\circ}}</span>.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc10/2022B/2025_01_28_64e1587309b5a3771dbdg-095.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-20"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> be a polynomial with rational coefficients such that when <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> is divided by the polynomial <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+x+1</span>, the remainder is <span class=\"tex\">x+2</span>, and when <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> is divided by the polynomial <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+1</span>, the remainder is <span class=\"tex\">2 x+1</span>. There is a unique polynomial of least degree with these two properties. What is the sum of the squares of the coefficients of that polynomial?</p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">10</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">13</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">19</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">20</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">23</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>Write <span class=\"tex\">P(x)=Q(x)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)+(x+2)</span> for some polynomial <span class=\"tex\">Q(x)</span> with rational coefficients. It follows that <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+1</span> divides</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(x)-(2 x+1)=Q(x)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)+x Q(x)-x+1</span></p><p>Thus <span class=\"tex\">x Q(x)-x+1</span> is a multiple of <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+1</span>. Hence the degree of <span class=\"tex\">Q(x)</span> is at least 1 . Assume that <span class=\"tex\">Q(x)=a x+b</span> and that <span class=\"tex\">x Q(x)-x+1=c\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)</span> for some rational numbers <span class=\"tex\">a, b</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">c</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">c=1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">a=b=1</span>. Therefore the polynomial of least degree is</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(x)=(x+1)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)+(x+2)=x^{3}+2 x^{2}+3 x+3</span></p><p>The requested sum of squares is <span class=\"tex\">1^{2}+2^{2}+3^{2}+3^{2}=(\\text{E})\\boxed{23}</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\textbf{OR}</span></p><p>Divide <span class=\"tex\">\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)</span> into <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> to obtain a quotient polynomial <span class=\"tex\">Q(x)</span> and a remainder <span class=\"tex\">R(x)</span>. Thus</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(x)=Q(x)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)+R(x)</span></p><p>When <span class=\"tex\">R(x)</span> is divided by <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+x+1</span> it gives the same remainder, <span class=\"tex\">x+2</span>, as does <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span>, and when <span class=\"tex\">R(x)</span> is divided by <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+1</span> it gives the same remainder, <span class=\"tex\">2 x+1</span>, as does <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span>. But <span class=\"tex\">R(x)</span> has degree at most 3 , so the polynomial of least degree having the desired properties has degree at most 3 . Let <span class=\"tex\">P(x)=a_{3} x^{3}+a_{2} x^{2}+a_{1} x+a_{0}</span> where the values of the coefficients <span class=\"tex\">a_{i}</span> are to be determined. Polynomial division yields</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &amp; =\\left(a_{3} x+a_{2}-a_{3}\\right)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)+\\left(a_{1}-a_{2}\\right) x+a_{0}-a_{2}+a_{3} \\\\\n&amp; =\\left(a_{3} x+a_{2}\\right)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)+\\left(a_{1}-a_{3}\\right) x+a_{0}-a_{2}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>For <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> to have the desired properties, the <span class=\"tex\">a_{i}</span> must be chosen so that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\n&amp; a_{1}-a_{2}=1 \\\\\n&amp; a_{0} \\quad-a_{2}+a_{3}=2 \\\\\n&amp; a_{1} \\quad-a_{3}=2 \\\\\n&amp; a_{0}-a_{2}=1 .\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>To solve this system, first subtract the fourth equation from the second to see that <span class=\"tex\">a_{3}=1</span>. Then insert this information into the third equation to see that <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}=3</span>. Insert this value of <span class=\"tex\">a_{1}</span> in the first equation to see that <span class=\"tex\">a_{2}=2</span>. Finally, insert this value of <span class=\"tex\">a_{2}</span> into the fourth equation to see that <span class=\"tex\">a_{0}=3</span>. Thus <span class=\"tex\">P(x)=x^{3}+2 x^{2}+3 x+3</span>, as above.</p>\n<p>Let <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> be any polynomial that yields the required remainders. By polynomial division, there exist polynomials <span class=\"tex\">Q(x)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">R(x)</span> such that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(x)=Q(x)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)+R(x)</span></p><p>where <span class=\"tex\">R(x)</span> has degree less than or equal to 3 . The polynomial <span class=\"tex\">R(x)</span> also yields the two required remainders, and a polynomial that yields the required remainders and has degree less than or equal to 3 is unique. Indeed, if there were two of them, say <span class=\"tex\">R_{1}(x)</span> and <span class=\"tex\">R_{2}(x)</span>, then put <span class=\"tex\">S(x)=</span> <span class=\"tex\">R_{1}(x)-R_{2}(x)</span>. Both <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+x+1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+1</span> evenly divide <span class=\"tex\">S(x)</span>. Because the two divisors are relatively prime, their product (which has degree 4) divides <span class=\"tex\">S(x)</span>. Because <span class=\"tex\">S(x)</span> has degree less than or equal to 3 , this is possible only if <span class=\"tex\">S(x)</span> is identically zero.<br/>\nBecause <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> yields the desired remainder when divided by <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+x+1</span>, which is to say that there is a polynomial <span class=\"tex\">Q_{1}(x)</span> such that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">P(x)=Q_{1}(x)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)+x+2 \\tag{1}</span></p><p>It suffices to find all <span class=\"tex\">Q_{1}(x)</span> such that there exists a polynomial <span class=\"tex\">Q_{2}(x)</span> such that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">Q_{1}(x)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)+x+2=Q_{2}(x)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)+2 x+1</span></p><p>that is,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">Q_{1}(x)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)=Q_{2}(x)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)+x-1 \\tag{2}</span></p><p>A brief application of the Extended Euclidean Algorithm yields</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">-x\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)+(x+1)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)=1</span></p><p>Multiply both sides of this by <span class=\"tex\">x-1</span>, and rearrange, to see that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(-x^{2}+x\\right)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)=\\left(1-x^{2}\\right)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)+x-1</span></p><p>Subtract the respective sides of this from equation (2) to see that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\left(Q_{1}(x)+x^{2}-x\\right)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)=\\left(Q_{2}(x)+x^{2}-1\\right)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)</span></p><p>Because <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+x+1</span> and <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+1</span> are relatively prime, it follows that <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+1</span> divides the first factor on the left-hand side above. That is, there is a polynomial <span class=\"tex\">K(x)</span> such that <span class=\"tex\">Q_{1}(x)=-x^{2}+x+K(x)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)</span>. For <span class=\"tex\">Q_{1}(x)</span> of this form, the polynomial <span class=\"tex\">P(x)</span> will have the desired remainder when divided by <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+1</span>. Substitute this formula for <span class=\"tex\">Q_{1}(x)</span> into (1) to see that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &amp; =\\left(-x^{2}+x+K(x)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)\\right)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)+x+2 \\\\\n&amp; =-x^{4}+2 x+2+K(x)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>The desired polynomial of least degree occurs when <span class=\"tex\">K(x)=1</span> - that is, when <span class=\"tex\">P(x)=x^{3}+2 x^{2}+</span> <span class=\"tex\">3 x+3</span>, as above. As a quick check,</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3}+2 x^{2}+3 x+3 &amp; =(x+1)\\left(x^{2}+x+1\\right)+x+2 \\\\\n&amp; =(x+2)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)+2 x+1\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>Note: The calculation completed in the third solution generalizes to give an analogue for polynomials of the Chinese Remainder Theorem.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-21"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">S</span> be the set of circles in the coordinate plane that are tangent to each of the three circles with equations <span class=\"tex\">x^{2}+y^{2}=4, x^{2}+y^{2}=64</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">(x-5)^{2}+y^{2}=3</span>. What is the sum of the areas of all circles in <span class=\"tex\">S?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">48 \\pi</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">68 \\pi</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">96 \\pi</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">102 \\pi</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">136 \\pi</span>"], "a": "E", "s": "<p>Note that the given circles are centered at <span class=\"tex\">(0,0),(0,0)</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">(5,0)</span> and have radii 2,8 , and <span class=\"tex\">\\sqrt{3}</span>, respectively. Label these circles <span class=\"tex\">I, O</span>, and <span class=\"tex\">M</span>, respectively. Let <span class=\"tex\">C</span> be a circle in <span class=\"tex\">S</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">C</span> must be internally tangent to the circle of radius 8 . There are two cases.</p>\n<p>If circle <span class=\"tex\">I</span> is externally tangent to <span class=\"tex\">C</span>, then the diameter of <span class=\"tex\">C</span> is <span class=\"tex\">8-2=6</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">C</span> has radius 3 . Imagine <span class=\"tex\">C</span> rotating around the origin, with its center <span class=\"tex\">2+3=5</span> units from the origin. There are two positions in which circle <span class=\"tex\">M</span> is externally tangent to <span class=\"tex\">C</span> and two positions in which <span class=\"tex\">M</span> is internally tangent to <span class=\"tex\">C</span>. This gives four circles of radius 3 that meet the conditions of the problem, as shown below.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc10/2022B/2025_01_28_64e1587309b5a3771dbdg-098.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>If circle <span class=\"tex\">I</span> is internally tangent to <span class=\"tex\">C</span>, then the diameter of <span class=\"tex\">C</span> is <span class=\"tex\">8+2=10</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">C</span> has radius 5 . Imagine <span class=\"tex\">C</span> rotating around the origin, with its center <span class=\"tex\">5-2=3</span> units from the origin. There are two positions in which circle <span class=\"tex\">M</span> is externally tangent to <span class=\"tex\">C</span> and two positions in which <span class=\"tex\">M</span> is internally tangent to <span class=\"tex\">C</span>. This gives four circles of radius 5 that meet the conditions of the problem, as shown below.</p>\n<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img alt=\"Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/amc10/2022B/2025_01_28_64e1587309b5a3771dbdg-099.jpg\" width=\"500\"/></div>\n<p>The total area of these eight circles is <span class=\"tex\">4 \\pi \\cdot 3^{2}+4 \\pi \\cdot 5^{2}=(\\text{E})\\boxed{136 \\pi}</span>.</p>\n<p>Note: Problems like this one were studied by the ancient Greek mathematician and astronomer Apollonius of Perga.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-22"}, {"q": "<p> Ant Amelia starts on the number line at <span class=\"tex\">0</span> and crawls in the following manner. For <span class=\"tex\">n=1,2,3</span>, Amelia chooses a time duration <span class=\"tex\">t_{n}</span> and an increment <span class=\"tex\">x_{n}</span> independently and uniformly at random from the interval <span class=\"tex\">(0,1)</span>. During the <span class=\"tex\">n</span>th step of the process, Amelia moves <span class=\"tex\">x_{n}</span> units in the positive direction, using up <span class=\"tex\">t_{n}</span> minutes. If the total elapsed time has exceeded <span class=\"tex\">1</span> minute during the <span class=\"tex\">n</span>th step, she stops at the end of that step; otherwise, she continues with the next step, taking at most <span class=\"tex\">3</span> steps in all. What is the probability that Amelia's position when she stops will be greater than <span class=\"tex\">1?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{1}{2}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{2}{3}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{3}{4}</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">\\dfrac{5}{6}</span>"], "a": "C", "s": "<p>The conditions of the problem guarantee that Amelia stops after either 2 steps or 3 steps. The probability that she will stop after 2 steps is the probability that <span class=\"tex\">t_{1}+t_{2}&gt;1</span>. This can be computed as the area of the triangular region in the square <span class=\"tex\">[0,1] \\times[0,1]</span> in the <span class=\"tex\">\\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\\right)</span>-coordinate plane that lies above the line <span class=\"tex\">t_{1}+t_{2}=1</span>, which is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2}</span>. If Amelia stops after 2 steps, the probability that her position is greater than 1 is, by the same reasoning with <span class=\"tex\">x_{1}</span> and <span class=\"tex\">x_{2}</span>, equal to <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2}</span>.</p>\n<p>Otherwise, Amelia stops after 3 steps, and this happens with probability <span class=\"tex\">1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}</span>. In this case the probability that her position is greater than 1 is equal to the probability that <span class=\"tex\">x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}&gt;1</span>. This can be computed as the volume of the region in the cube <span class=\"tex\">[0,1] \\times[0,1] \\times[0,1]</span> in the <span class=\"tex\">\\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}\\right)</span> coordinate space that lies above the plane <span class=\"tex\">x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}=1</span>. The complement of this region is a pyramid with isosceles right triangular base with leg length 1 and altitude 1 , so its volume is <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{6}</span>. It follows that the probability that Amelia's position if she stops after the third step will be greater than 1 is <span class=\"tex\">1-\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{5}{6}</span>.</p>\n<p>Combining both cases yields an answer of <span class=\"tex\">\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{6}=(\\text{C})\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-23"}, {"q": "<p> Consider functions <span class=\"tex\">f</span> that satisfy</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">|f(x)-f(y)| \\leq \\dfrac{1}{2}|x-y|</span></p><p>for all real numbers <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span>. Of all such functions that also satisfy the equation <span class=\"tex\">f(300)=f(900)</span>, what is the greatest possible value of</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">f(f(800))-f(f(400))?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">25</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">50</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">100</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">150</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">200</span>"], "a": "B", "s": "<p>Note that</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">\\begin{aligned}\nf(f(800))-f(f(400)) &amp; \\leq|f(f(800))-f(f(400))| \\\\\n&amp; \\leq \\frac{1}{2}|f(800)-f(400)| \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{1}{2}|f(800)-f(900)+f(900)-f(300)+f(300)-f(400)| \\\\\n&amp; \\leq \\frac{1}{2}(|f(800)-f(900)|+|f(900)-f(300)|+|f(300)-f(400)|) \\\\\n&amp; \\leq \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 100+0+\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 100\\right)=(\\text{B})\\boxed{50}\n\\end{aligned}</span></p><p>This maximum is achieved, for example, by the function</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">f(x)=-\\frac{|300-|500-x||+800}{2}</span></p><p>Note: A function <span class=\"tex\">f</span> for which <span class=\"tex\">|f(x)-f(y)| \\leq c|x-y|</span> for all real numbers <span class=\"tex\">x</span> and <span class=\"tex\">y</span> is known as a Lipschitz function with constant <span class=\"tex\">c</span>.</p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-24"}, {"q": "<p> Let <span class=\"tex\">x_{0}, x_{1}, x_{2}, \\cdots</span> be a sequence of numbers, where each <span class=\"tex\">x_{k}</span> is either <span class=\"tex\">0</span> or <span class=\"tex\">1</span>. For each positive integer <span class=\"tex\">n</span>, define</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">S_{n}=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} x_{k} 2^{k}</span></p><p>Suppose <span class=\"tex\">7 S_{n} \\equiv 1\\left(\\bmod 2^{n}\\right)</span> for all <span class=\"tex\">n \\geq 1</span>. What is the value of the sum</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">x_{2019}+2 x_{2020}+4 x_{2021}+8 x_{2022}?</span></p>", "c": ["<span class=\"tex\">6</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">7</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">12</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">14</span>", "<span class=\"tex\">15</span>"], "a": "A", "s": "<p>The binary representation for <span class=\"tex\">S_{n}</span> is the digit string</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">x_{n-1} x_{n-2} x_{n-3} \\ldots x_{0}</span></p><p>The first few rightmost digits can be computed from the given congruence. Indeed, <span class=\"tex\">7 S_{1} \\equiv 1</span> <span class=\"tex\">(\\bmod 2)</span> implies <span class=\"tex\">x_{0}=1</span>. Then <span class=\"tex\">7 S_{2} \\equiv 1(\\bmod 4)</span> implies</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1 \\equiv 3 x_{0}+3 x_{1} \\cdot 2 \\equiv 3+2 x_{1}(\\bmod 4)</span></p><p>from which <span class=\"tex\">x_{1}=1</span>. Next, <span class=\"tex\">7 S_{3} \\equiv 1(\\bmod 8)</span> implies</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1 \\equiv 7 x_{0}+7 x_{1} \\cdot 2+7 x_{2} \\cdot 4 \\equiv 7+6+4 x_{2}(\\bmod 8)</span></p><p>from which <span class=\"tex\">x_{2}=1</span>. Finally, <span class=\"tex\">7 S_{4} \\equiv 1(\\bmod 16)</span> implies</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">1 \\equiv 7 x_{0}+7 x_{1} \\cdot 2+7 x_{2} \\cdot 4+7 x_{3} \\cdot 8 \\equiv 7+14+12+8 x_{3}(\\bmod 16)</span></p><p>from which <span class=\"tex\">x_{3}=0</span>.\\<br/>\nFor <span class=\"tex\">n \\geq 3</span>, the rightmost 4 binary digits of <span class=\"tex\">S_{n}+1</span> are 1000 , as are the rightmost 4 binary digits of <span class=\"tex\">8 S_{n}</span>. Now write</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">7 S_{n}-1=8 S_{n}-\\left(S_{n}+1\\right)</span></p><p>and note that because <span class=\"tex\">7 S_{n}-1</span> is a multiple of <span class=\"tex\">2^{n}</span>, the rightmost <span class=\"tex\">n</span> binary digits of <span class=\"tex\">7 S_{n}-1</span> are all 0 . But, for <span class=\"tex\">1 \\leq k \\leq n-4</span>, the <span class=\"tex\">(k+4)</span> th binary digit from the right of <span class=\"tex\">8 S_{n}-\\left(S_{n}+1\\right)</span> is <span class=\"tex\">x_{k}-x_{k+3}</span>, so <span class=\"tex\">x_{k}-x_{k+3}=0</span>. It follows that <span class=\"tex\">x_{k+3}=x_{k}</span> for <span class=\"tex\">k \\geq 1</span>. Therefore</p>\n<p><span class=\"tex\" data-display=\"\">x_{2019}+2 x_{2020}+4 x_{2021}+8 x_{2022}=x_{3}+2 x_{1}+4 x_{2}+8 x_{3}=0+2 \\cdot 1+4 \\cdot 1+8 \\cdot 0=(\\text{A})\\boxed{6}</span></p>", "sol": "https://wiki.randommath.com/amc10/2022/part-b/problem-25"}]});
